<header><p class="st-kicker">princely state · Indian princely states</p><h1 class="st-title">Sambalpur State</h1><p class="st-meta">1493 CE – 1849 CE (approx.) · capital Sambalpur</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Chauhan Rajput kingdom on the middle Mahanadi in western Odisha, whose annexation by the British in 1849 led to the long rebellion of Surendra Sai.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Tradition holds that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balaram-deva">Balaram Deva</a>, a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> ruling family of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/patna-state">Patna State</a>, founded a separate kingdom at Sambalpur at the end of the 15th century. It soon became the leading power among the chiefs of the region.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Sambalpur controlled the diamond-bearing reaches of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahanadi">Mahanadi</a> and was known for its weaving and its goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaleswari">Samaleswari</a>. In the late 18th century it fell to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhonsles">Bhonsles</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagpur-kingdom">Nagpur Kingdom</a>. The British took it in 1803 and restored a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chauhan">Chauhan</a> ruler under their supervision.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>When Raja Narayan Singh died without a male heir in 1849, the British annexed Sambalpur. A claimant, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/surendra-sai">Surendra Sai</a>, led an armed rebellion from 1857 until his surrender in 1862. Sambalpur is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odisha">Odisha</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sambalpur-state.jpg" alt="Sambalpur State" width="320" height="241"><figcaption>Image: Government of British India · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:SonpurKart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1493 CE – 1849 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>princely state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sambalpur</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Balaram Deva, Narayan Singh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sambalpuri</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sambalpur</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1671&amp;focus=sambalpur-state" aria-label="Sambalpur State on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6935 1384 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sambalpur State lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7255 1640L6991 1757M7255 1640L7364 1601"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6991 1757h0M7364 1601h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7255 1640h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7255 1640h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1671&amp;focus=sambalpur-state">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

